Z||000000||fiero56||10-27-2000||05:57 PM||||Still trying to get my '86 SE v-6 running right. <P>My question is: is it possible to get a general idea of how much a timing chain is stretched, without pulling the cover and everything off? Particularly, could I put a wrench on the crank pulley bolt, and watch the rotor on the distributor, and see how many degrees the crank will move before the cam moves the distributor? I'm not looking for an exact measurement, but it would help me eliminate/confirm one possible problem.||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||10-27-2000||06:26 PM||||Yep.  The easiest way I know of, is to remove all the plugs, then turn crank CCW (with a socket+ breaker bar).  Then turn it CW until it goes "clank."<P>The amount the crank rotates until the "clank," is the number of degrees of slop in the chain. ||||reg||3
